  "query_preview": "..Dry, southwesterly flow across the region prevails\nthrough Thursday on the leading edge of a longwave trough\ncurrently situated off the west coast. This will keep dry and\nseasonably cool conditions in place through this period.\n\nA shift in the pattern is expected on Friday as moist, southerly\nflow strengthens across the region as a northern stream low\npressure system begins to phase into a tropical low off the Baja\nPeninsula. Concurrently, a shortwave trough ejecting \nnortheastward on the eastern periphery of the parent trough will \ntraverse our region through the day, introducing more substantial \ninstability especially across southwest and west- central Utah on \nFriday afternoon. The combination of this tropical moisture influx\nwith increasing instability will allow scattered showers and \nthunderstorms to develop by late Friday morning through the \nevening, with the main threat for heavy rainfall and resultant \nflash flooding focused where instability is maximized across \nsouthwest and west-central Utah. There is a considerable forecast \nbust scenario that still exists in models, as around 33% of \nmembers suggest the northern stream trough remaining decoupled\nfrom the tropical low, which would keep dry, southwesterly flow in\nplace through this period. This lower end scenario would\nessentially squash the flash flood risk on Friday, so keep an eye\non the forecast as we continue to monitor this potential.\n\nA brief period of subsidence is expected behind the exiting\nshortwave on Saturday, with shower activity gradually tapering off\nSunday morning. A relative lull is expected through the day, but\nwill be brief as our next, likely wetter, system begins to move\ninto the region. The northern stream trough and tropical low are\nexpected to shift inland late Saturday. This will transport more\nsubstantial amounts of moisture into the region given the tropical\nlow tapping into a strong atmospheric river, allowing widespread\nprecipitation to begin filling in from the south as early as late\nSaturday. Models have started to converge on northern Utah tapping\ninto more considerable precipitation, mainly due to a strong cold\nfront passage as the northern stream trough digs into the Great\nBasin on Sunday. With northwesterly flow developing behind the\nfront and much colder temperatures aloft, potential for lake \nenhanced precipitation will also come into the mix late Sunday\ninto Monday morning. While widespread heavy precipitation is\nlikely late Saturday through Monday morning, there still is a\nnotable spread in potential accumulation amounts. For most areas,\nthe 25th percentile indicates widespread totals in the 0.2-0.5\"\nrange, while the 75th percentile indicates totals between\n0.75-1.25\". For southwest Utah, the Wasatch Front, and all Utah \nmountains, higher totals are favored across the percentile ranges,\nwith the 25th percentile indicating accumulations between 0.5-1\",\nand 75th percentile between 1.5-2.5\". \n\nBehind the cold front late Sunday, snow levels are expected to\ndrop as low as 6500-7000 feet by early Monday morning. With\nlingering showers expected to continue through this period,\nespecially downstream of the GSL from lake enhancement,\nprecipitation is expected to transition to snow and bring notable\naccumulations especially to areas above 8000 feet. Current\nforecast shows generally 3-6 inches above 8000 feet, with higher\ntotals between 8-14 inches above 9000 feet.\n\nPrecipitation looks to taper off through Monday morning, with\ndrier conditions prevailing through the remainder of the day.\nLow temperatures will be some of the coolest of the season so far \nearly next week.",
